WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.780 --> 00:00:05.760
As we celebrate the golden anniversary of
the Protein Data Bank, let’s look at the

00:00:05.760 --> 00:00:09.660
history and the milestones that shaped it
to become the essential resource for research

00:00:09.660 --> 00:00:12.620
and education it is today.

00:00:12.620 --> 00:00:15.330
The Protein Data Bank was established in 1971.

00:00:15.330 --> 00:00:22.730
In its first years the archive was very small
and contained a few groundbreaking structures.

00:00:22.730 --> 00:00:27.120
For each structure, the PDB contained the
three-dimensional coordinates, or the x, y,

00:00:27.120 --> 00:00:31.850
and z positions of each atom.

00:00:31.850 --> 00:00:33.970
How did the scientists “see” the atoms?

00:00:33.970 --> 00:00:40.329
Back in the 1970s, the sole method to do so
was X-ray crystallography.

00:00:40.329 --> 00:00:44.499
Proteins form crystals when they are purified
and concentrated.

00:00:44.499 --> 00:00:48.917
Within the crystal, many copies of the protein
are arranged in symmetrical arrays.

00:00:50.920 --> 00:00:54.809
When an x-ray beam strikes the crystal, the
x-rays scatter into a distinctive pattern

00:00:54.809 --> 00:00:56.940
of spots.

00:00:56.940 --> 00:01:00.570
These x-ray diffraction patterns are then
analyzed to determine the positions of atoms

00:01:00.570 --> 00:01:02.334
in the protein.

00:01:04.370 --> 00:01:09.110
In 1913, the father-son team, Henry and Lawrence
Bragg were the first to describe how to interpret

00:01:09.110 --> 00:01:13.000
the X-ray diffraction patterns.

00:01:19.030 --> 00:01:23.000
They used a sodium chloride or salt crystal
for their experiments.

00:01:23.000 --> 00:01:27.850
Later, the Braggs mentored many world class
researchers who used X-ray crystallography

00:01:27.850 --> 00:01:32.792
on a variety of increasingly complex substances.

00:01:38.829 --> 00:01:43.630
In 1958, using X-ray crystallography, John
Kendrew determined the first atomic structure

00:01:43.630 --> 00:01:45.320
of a protein, myoglobin.

00:01:45.320 --> 00:01:49.610
With that, the field of structural biology
was born.

00:01:49.610 --> 00:01:53.560
More and more scientists started to study
the 3D structures of proteins and by the early

00:01:53.560 --> 00:01:58.600
and by the early 1970's, there were a dozen atomic structures
solved in different labs.

00:01:58.600 --> 00:02:03.270
Researchers around the world were interested
in exploring what these 3D structures revealed.

00:02:03.270 --> 00:02:07.039
However, data sharing was difficult in the
time before the Internet.

00:02:07.039 --> 00:02:11.650
Early PDB structures were stored on paper
punch cards, each containing information about

00:02:11.650 --> 00:02:13.760
1 atom.

00:02:13.760 --> 00:02:17.090
The Protein Data Bank archive was established
to solve this problem.

00:02:17.090 --> 00:02:20.599
The scientists deposited the data to the PDB
and the PDB copied the data onto magnetic

00:02:20.599 --> 00:02:23.620
tapes and distributed them to researchers
upon request.

00:02:23.620 --> 00:02:25.400
In October 1980, 10 years after the PDB was
established, there were more than 150 structures

00:02:25.400 --> 00:02:26.802
in the archive available for distribution.

00:02:26.802 --> 00:02:32.260
In October 1980, 10 years after the PDB was
established, the PDB archive holdings contained

00:02:32.260 --> 00:02:36.340
151 structures available for distribution.

00:02:36.340 --> 00:02:42.400
A year later, the first DNA structure was
added to the PDB.

00:02:42.400 --> 00:02:46.590
In 1989, the first structure determined by
a biophysical method called Nuclear Magnetic

00:02:46.590 --> 00:02:52.049
Resonance Spectroscopy or NMR was deposited
to the PDB.

00:02:52.049 --> 00:02:56.520
In this method, a purified protein is mixed
with special solvent and inserted into an

00:02:56.520 --> 00:02:58.540
NMR probe.

00:02:58.540 --> 00:03:07.370
The protein sample is then exposed to a strong
magnetic field.

00:03:07.370 --> 00:03:12.080
This makes the nuclei of certain atoms, like
hydrogen, spin like tops.

00:03:12.080 --> 00:03:17.530
When the sample is probed with radio waves,
the nuclei become excited and resonate.

00:03:17.530 --> 00:03:20.709
The frequencies are measured and recorded.

00:03:20.709 --> 00:03:26.090
The surroundings of the atoms determine how
high, or how low the frequencies are.

00:03:26.090 --> 00:03:30.040
Using computational methods, the measurements
are converted into a graph that represents

00:03:30.040 --> 00:03:35.440
the frequencies as peaks with specific locations
for specific atom groups.

00:03:35.440 --> 00:03:39.840
This information is further refined and combined
with additional NMR experiments to determine

00:03:39.840 --> 00:03:41.170
the 3D structure.

00:03:41.170 --> 00:03:46.439
The NMR method proved to be effective in solving
3D structures of smaller proteins and gave

00:03:46.439 --> 00:03:51.239
us a unique glimpse of how proteins behave
in solution.

00:03:51.239 --> 00:03:57.260
In 1991, the first structure determined using
3D Electron Microscopy or 3DEM was released

00:03:57.260 --> 00:03:59.099
by the PDB.

00:03:59.099 --> 00:04:05.370
In this method, a tiny amount of purified
protein is placed onto a copper grid.

00:04:05.370 --> 00:04:12.610
A special machine spreads the sample in a
single layer on the grid.

00:04:12.610 --> 00:04:18.930
The sample is frozen in liquid ethane and
then loaded into an electron microscope.

00:04:18.930 --> 00:04:26.770
The sample is exposed to a beam of accelerated
electrons and images are captured.

00:04:26.770 --> 00:04:35.770
Usually, the proteins are in many different
orientations.

00:04:35.770 --> 00:04:39.970
The images are then grouped by orientation
and then combined computationally to reconstruct

00:04:39.970 --> 00:04:46.070
the 3D shape of a molecule.

00:04:46.070 --> 00:04:50.780
Atoms are fit into the map to derive the 3D
structure of the protein.

00:04:50.780 --> 00:04:54.550
3DEM has become a perfect complement to X-ray
and NMR techniques.

00:04:54.550 --> 00:04:58.400
The method is very effective on large, complex
assemblies that are typically difficult to

00:04:58.400 --> 00:05:01.240
study by either of the two older methods.

00:05:01.240 --> 00:05:06.380
In 1995, the PDB Archive became accessible
for the first time through a www website.

00:05:06.380 --> 00:05:12.870
The PDB holdings were accessible online through
various services since 1988.

00:05:12.870 --> 00:05:19.590
However, the new website equipped with a browser
marked a new chapter in data distribution.

00:05:19.590 --> 00:05:24.780
Researchers could still request the full archive
on CD ROM.

00:05:24.780 --> 00:05:28.792
In 1999, the 10,000th structure was released
into the PDB.

00:05:28.792 --> 00:05:33.380
The distribution of the PDB archive on physical
media was discontinued in 2004.

00:05:33.380 --> 00:05:37.720
By then the Internet and computer technology
made it possible for all data operations to

00:05:37.720 --> 00:05:42.220
be done completely online through the FTP
server and web portals maintained by the members

00:05:42.220 --> 00:05:45.490
of the newly established worldwide PDB organization.

00:05:45.490 --> 00:05:52.340
In 2008, the PDB archive reached another milestone
number of entries: 50,000 structures.

00:05:52.340 --> 00:05:56.560
In 2014, the number doubled.

00:05:56.560 --> 00:06:04.139
As the PDB entered its golden year, there
were more than 170,000 structures in the archive.

00:06:04.139 --> 00:06:08.669
The growing numbers of 3D structures of proteins
and nucleic acids further our understanding

00:06:08.669 --> 00:06:13.090
of biology and help us explore how life works
at the atomic level.

00:06:13.090 --> 00:06:18.830
Many Nobel Prizes have been awarded for projects
involving molecular structures.

00:06:18.830 --> 00:06:23.880
Structural biology research is essential in
addressing major public health emergencies.

00:06:23.880 --> 00:06:28.530
For example, scientists have used the structures
of HIV proteins to design drugs that stop

00:06:28.530 --> 00:06:31.850
the virus from multiplying.

00:06:31.850 --> 00:06:41.430
Today, as the world faces the COVID pandemic,
structural biologists are exploring its viral

00:06:41.430 --> 00:06:46.830
proteins in a quest for new therapeutics and
vaccines.

00:06:46.830 --> 00:06:51.271
The PDB provides ready access to structural
biology research ensuring the quality of the

00:06:51.271 --> 00:06:57.050
data and providing tools for query, visualization
and analysis of these structures.

00:06:57.050 --> 00:07:02.840
Now, 50 years since the PDB’s founding,
the Protein Data Bank continues to be a leading

00:07:02.840 --> 00:07:07.349
global resource for experimental data that
is playing a central role in scientific discovery

00:07:07.349 --> 00:07:08.210
and education.

